      <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
      <div class="mt-16">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
          Common Driveway Sealing Timing Mistakes
        </h2>

        <p>
          Sealer chemistry is temperature and moisture dependent, so the date on the calendar matters as much as the product in the pail.
        </p>

        <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
          <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Going by Air Temperature Instead of Surface Temperature</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
              A 60°F morning can leave a shaded driveway sitting in the low 50s, and pavement in full sun can be 40°F above the air. Sealer cares about the surface. Check it with an infrared thermometer or the back of your hand rather than trusting the forecast number.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Too Late in the Season</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
              Sealer needs warm days and mild nights to cure fully, and a coat applied in late autumn often gets a hard frost before it has hardened. That produces a soft, easily damaged film that fails over the winter. If overnight lows are heading toward 50°F, wait for spring.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Overnight Lows and Dew</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
              Curing does not stop at sunset. A cold night or heavy dew on a freshly sealed surface leaves streaks and soft spots, even after a warm afternoon. Check the overnight forecast for the 24 hours after application, not just the daytime high.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing New Asphalt in Its First Season</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
              Fresh asphalt needs six to twelve months to cure and release its volatile oils. Sealing within that window traps them, leaving pavement that stays soft and scuffs under tyres. New driveways should get through a full summer before the first coat goes down.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Closing -->
      <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          The <strong class="text-white">best time to seal a driveway</strong> is late spring through early autumn, on a dry day with surface temperatures between roughly 50°F and 90°F and overnight lows staying mild. That window matters more than the brand of sealer.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
          Work backwards from the forecast: two dry days ahead, a cool morning start so the surface is not baking by mid-application, and no frost or heavy dew that night. And on new asphalt, wait out the first full summer — the single most common timing mistake in this whole category.
        </p>
